<<~ ahu #head >>

! The Dev Loop ~ idempotent cures after a code change

A Lararium vessel boots from ''compiled dist'', founds from a ''baked genesis seed'', and runs from
''vessel state in the operator's home''. After editing code, three things can be stale — the dist,
the genesis blob, the on-disk vessel — and each has a cure. The cures are ''idempotent'': safe to
run from any prior state (running · stale · none), so the loop never needs a clean-slate ritual.

''The one to reach for after editing code:'' `lares vessel flow refresh`.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #commands >>

!! The reach taxonomy ~ which cure, when

Ordered by how much they touch — reach for the //least// that fixes your case:

<<~ranks reach serve -> reconcile -> rebuild -> refresh -> fresh -> reset -> wake >>

\procedure ~Cure(~Type:"" ~Params:"") ~Cure <<~Type>> <<~holds `[<~Params>]`>>

<<~Cure refresh "after/CODE-CHANGED does/`pnpm -r build` THEN reconcile --fresh — recompile dist + re-pave + re-bake genesis + serve ~ THE post-edit cure" >>
<<~Cure reconcile "after/just-converge does/stop the incumbent on the port (graceful→force, by port-access, no PID file) + [--fresh] wipe + serve ~ no rebuild" >>
<<~Cure rebuild "after/DEP-BUMP does/re-bake the genesis engine under current deps + serve ~ NO wipe, identity-safe (the serde-skew cure)" >>
<<~Cure fresh "after/re-found does/reset --force THEN serve ~ assumes dist already current" >>
<<~Cure serve "after/boot-only does/run the dist node, fail-fast ~ no convergence, no rebuild" >>
<<~Cure reset "after/re-pave does/wipe vessel storage + bootstrap → re-bake genesis → init ~ identity PRESERVED; --force required" >>
<<~Cure wake "after/standup does/idempotent entry: attach if healthy else start detached + hydrate; --init founds, --claude/--codex/--copilot/--vscode wire the harness MCP+hooks" >>

''The gap `refresh` closes:'' every other dev-loop verb assumes the dist is current — none run
`pnpm -r build`. The island workers spawn from compiled `.js` siblings (a tsx-source run = a
half-dead vessel: port bound, worker `ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND`). So after a TS edit, `serve`/`fresh`/
`reconcile` boot a STALE vessel. `refresh` = build first, then re-pave + serve — the complete cure.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #vessel-paths >>

!! Where state lives ~ vessel-home vs corpus (the ~/.lares split)

The CORPUS + code live in the ''repo'' (versioned); the vessel's RUNTIME STATE lives in the
operator's ''home'' (`~/.lares`). This is the `@daemon` ontology made filesystem — the
bound-operator's vessel-only worker belongs to the operator, not a checkout.

<<~ranks home ~/.lares/.lararium:storage·key·socket -> .lararium-identity:keypair -> .lararium-projection:synced-tree -> harvest·harvest-stage:memories >>
<<~ranks corpus repo/genesis:baked-island.bin-seed+bootstrap -> repo/bags:seed-memes -> repo/packages:code >>

''The resolver'' — ONE source, `@lararium/node/vessel-paths.ts` (`larHome` · `larDataDir` ·
`larIdentityDir` · `larProjectionDir` · `larHarvest*`), so the CLI (local-connector) and the daemon
(uds-channel) always agree on the ''UDS socket'' path. The palace stays `~/.mempalace`
(mempalace's own home; `MEMPALACE_PALACE_PATH` overrides).

''`LAR_ROOT`'' overrides the home root for ISOLATED instances (the test harness / staged pairs) —
both storage AND genesis fall under it. ''Only an EXPLICIT `--root` (or the env) sets it'' — never a
default; a `REPO_ROOT` default would resolve `larHome()` to the repo and defeat the split.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #laws >>

!! The invariants the loop rests on

<<~ranks law dist-not-tsx -> genesis-before-init -> identity-survives-reset -> only-explicit-LAR_ROOT -> deterministic-genesis >>

* ''dist, not tsx'' — island workers spawn compiled; a stale dist is a half-dead vessel. `refresh` rebuilds.
* ''genesis before init'' — init founds the hearth FROM the engine CID, so the baked artifact must
  exist first. `reset` order is delete → ''rebuild genesis'' → init (the reverse fails "engine CID absent").
* ''identity survives reset'' — `~/.lares/.lararium-identity` is a sibling of the wipe-zone; `reset`
  preserves the operator's keypair/DID. A true re-found (new DID) needs an explicit identity wipe.
* ''only explicit `--root` sets `LAR_ROOT`'' — never thread a `REPO_ROOT` default through reset/init
  (it silently relocates the vessel into the repo).
* ''deterministic genesis'' — a re-bake with unchanged code is byte-identical (no churn); a diff means
  the engine/plugin blob genuinely changed.

<<~/ahu >>

